# security.md compliance signal — no hardcoded secrets.
#
# Patterns below match common secret-bearing literals. The .compliance.regex
# format is conservative — false positives produce noise, so each line is
# anchored on a syntactic context that legitimate code rarely produces.
#
# Caveat: these patterns find the LITERAL leak, not the conceptual one.
# Use a real secret-scanner (gitleaks, trufflehog) for production gating;
# this signal is for trend visibility in the heartbeat log.

# Hardcoded API keys / tokens in Java/Kotlin/TS source: literal assignment
# of an OpenAI-style key.
=\s*"sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}"
=\s*"sk_live_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}"
=\s*"sk_test_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}"

# Bearer token literals (frequent copy-paste from curl examples).
=\s*"Bearer [A-Za-z0-9._-]{20,}"

# Slack/GitHub PATs.
xox[pborsa]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}
ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}

# Hardcoded password literal (matches `password = "<8+ chars>"`, common in
# fixtures that drift into prod).
\bpassword\s*=\s*"[^"\n]{8,}"
